title: 'D85 landed (approved round 2); first cross-OS CI: Ubuntu green, Windows parity failures triaged'
summary: |-
  PR-level: D85 is committed at 363862b1. Both reviewers approved in round 2 (rounds-to-pass: 2). Changes:
  - the cross-boot file-anchor lag relax is driven only by the keystore boot;
  - S34-24: keystore ahead plus file lag over 63 classifies as truncated with fileLag (also carried in the gap payload);
  - evidence first: no anchor rewrite happens before the signed lag row commits.
  Tests: gnomon 179, contracts 125. Aegis's S34-25 double-crash case is tracked as T-2026-10-17-012.

  CI: feat/gnomon was pushed and draft PR #49 opened so CI would run (the workflow runs only on PRs and main). Four CI rounds:
  1. Missing rolldown native binding.
  2. A second `npm install --no-save` pruned the first binding; fixed by installing both in one call.
  3. Windows CRLF broke the byte-equal schema check (fixed with an LF-pinned .gitattributes), and Ubuntu daemon tests made homes under world-writable /tmp, which the daemon correctly refuses (fixed with ~/.cache/gnomon-test).
  4. Ubuntu fully green. Windows has 83 failures across 6 files: directory fsync EPERM, ESM path imports, platform markers not enforced at runtime, and POSIX-path assumptions. These are tracked as T-2026-10-17-011, and a builder is on them.
symbols_touched:
  - '#kernel-log'
  - '#gnomon-ci'
  - $daemon-lifecycle
commit: 363862b1
learnings:
  - npm install --no-save prunes extraneous packages from earlier --no-save installs; install all platform binaries in one call.
  - 'The first Windows CI run is where ''macOS-first'' code meets reality: directory fsync is refused on Windows (EPERM).'
